iles/e01fb23/1856352463.pdf)\nciteturn34search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; 2012, 338, 506–510, DOI: 10.1126/science.1228638. ... quantification.de/real-time-pcr-handbook-life-technologies-update-flr.pdf, 2012.\nLiteratur \n3 \n \nLiteratur \n[1] G. P. Acuna, F. M. Möller, P. Holzmeister, S. Beater, B. Lalkens und P. Tinnefeld, \nScience (New York, N.Y.) 2012, 338, 506–510, DOI: 10.1126/science.1228638. \n[2] A. Puchkova, C. Vietz, E. Pibiri, B. Wünsch, M. Sanz Paz, G. P. Acuna und P. \nTinnefeld, Nano letters 2015, 15, 8354–8359, DOI: \n10.1021/acs.nanolett.5b04045. \n[3] V. Glembockyte, L. Grabenhorst, K. Trofymchuk und P. Tinnefeld, Accounts of \nChemical Research 2021, 54, 3338–3348, DOI: 10.1021/acs.accounts.1c00307. \n[4] S. E. Ochmann, C. Vietz, K. Trofymchuk, G. P. Acuna, B. Lalkens und P. Tinnefeld, \nAnalytical Chemistry 2017, 89, 13000–13007, DOI: \n10.1021/acs.analchem.7b04082. \n[5] P. W. K. Rothemund, Nature 2006, 440, 297–302, DOI: 10.1038/nature04586. \n[6] A. Brandenburg, F. Curdt, G. Sulz, F. Ebling, J. Nestler, K. Wunderlich und D. \nMichel, Sensors and Actuators B: Chemical 2009, 139, 245–251, DOI: \n10.1016/j.snb.2009.02.052. \n[7] M. Kemmler, B. Koger, G. Sulz, U. Sauer, E. Schleicher, C. Preininger und A. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFocusing light on the nanoscale: Making nanolenses from metallic particles and DNA (https://idw-online.de/de/news?id=503625&print=1)\nciteturn34search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; SCIENCE, Friday, 26 October 2012, DOI 10.1126/science.1228638\nFurther attachment sites between the nanoparticles were used to attach optical sources such as a fluorescent dye. Biocompatibility of the nanooptical devices was proven by the single-molecule detection of short nucleic acid diffusing in the solution. The functioning of the self-assembled nanolens was demonstrated by a drastic fluorescence enhancement by a factor of ~100 for single fluorescent molecules.\nThe scientists are confident that their technique might have an impact on a broad range of research disciplines. Prof. Dr. Philip Tinnefeld describes the extent of the possible applications enabled by their findings: “Concentrating the light into very reduced volume in the zeptoliter range allows us to perform studies on individual objects with better signals and at higher concentrations where biologically relevant processes like DNA replication occur. Additionally, we can now investigate how light interacts with nanoparticles, a key component for the field of nanophotonics”.\nThis work was funded by the European Research Council (ERC), the Volkswagen Foundation and the Center for NanoScience CeNS.\nPublication:\n“Fluorescence Enhancement at Docking Sites of DNA-Directed Self-Assembled Nanoantennas”. Guillermo Acuna, Friederike Möller, Phil Holzmeister, Susanne Beater, Birka Lalkens and Philip Tinnefeld. SCIENCE, Friday, 26 October 2012, DOI 10.1126/science.1228638\nContact:\nProf. Dr. Philip Tinnefeld.\nInstitut für Physikalische und Theoretische Chemie\nTechnische Universität Braunschweig\nHans-Sommer-Strasse 10\n38106 Braunschweig\np.tinnefeld@tu-braunschweig.de\nTel.: +49 531 391 5330\nDr. Guillermo Acuna\nInstitut für Physikalische und Theoretische Chemie\nTechnische Universität Braunschweig\nHans-Sommer-Strasse 10\n38106 Braunschweig\ng.acuna@tu-braunschweig.de\nTel.: +49 531 391 7377\n\n\nBilder\n\n<\nThe sketch shows the DNA origami nanopillar (in gray) immobilized on a coverslip. Two gold nanoparti ...\nQuelle: TU Braunschweig\n\n\nMerkmale dieser Pressemitteilung:\nJournalisten\nChemie\nüberregional\nForschungsergebnisse\nEnglisch\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFluorescence Enhancement at Docking Sites of DNA-Directed Self-Assembled Nanoantennas (https://agris.fao.org/search/en/providers/122535/records/65df9bc10f3e94b9e5da6f77)\nciteturn34search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; DOI DOI http://dx.doi.org/10.1126/science.1228638\n\n# Fluorescence Enhancement at Docking Sites of DNA-Directed Self-Assembled Nanoantennas\n\n## 2012\n\nAcuna, G. P. | Möller, F. M. | Holzmeister, P. | Beater, S. | Lalkens, B. | Tinnefeld, P.\n\nBuilding a Fluorescent Hotspot When two gold nanoparticles come close together, their overlapping plasmonic fields can create a region that acts as a nanoantenna that can enhance the fluorescent emission of a molecule. Acuna et al. (p. 506) used a surface-anchored DNA origami structure to assemble one or two gold nanoparticles next to a dye trapped within the structure. A > 100-fold enhancement in fluorescent emission was observed when the dye molecules were located in a 23-nm gap between two 100-nm gold nanoparticles.\n\n##### AGROVOC Keywords\n\ndna fluorescence\n\n##### Bibliographic information\n\nPublished in\n\nScience\n\nVolume 338 Issue 6106 Pagination 506 - 510 ISSN 0036-8075\n\nPublisher\n\nAmerican Association for the Advancement of Science\n\nOther Subjects\n\nNanogold\n\nLanguage\n\nEnglish\n\nType\n\nJournal Article; Text\n\nIn AGRIS since: 2024-02-28\n\nFormat: MODS\n\nData Provider\n\nThis bibliographic record has been provided by National Agricultural Library\n\nLinks\n\nDOI DOI http://dx.doi.org/10.1126/science.1228638\n\nLookup at Google Scholar--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nc\ns\no\nr\nu\ne\nN\ni\n \nn\ne\n \ne\nc\n \nd\nn\na\n \ni\nB\n \nom\n \ni (https://aaltodoc.aalto.fi/bitstreams/167b7ea6-de26-4d5d-a49f-2760f39bf01b/download)\nciteturn34search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; 338 (6106), 506–510. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1228638.\n(168) Acuna, G. P.; Möller, F. M.; Holzmeister, P.; Beater, S.; Lalkens, B.; Tinnefeld, P. Fluorescence \nEnhancement at Docking Sites of DNA-Directed Self-Assembled Nanoantennas. Science 2012, \n338 (6106), 506–510. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1228638. \n(169) Vietz, C.; Kamins